ure your response matches one of the valid moves exactly.\nRemember to provide Explanation, Reason, and Answer.", 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Evaluation: The last play was a six-card sequence: ♠9 ♥10 ♦J ♣Q ♦K ♥A. This is not a bomb or rocket, so it can only be beaten by a higher sequence of six cards — but such sequences are rare and difficult to form. Checking my hand, I do not have a legal sequence of six consecutive cards (and sequences cannot include 2s or jokers), so I cannot beat this play with a sequence. Additionally, no other combination type (like triplets with attachments or pairs) matches the six-card length in a way that can beat this specific play. The only moves available from the "Valid Moves" list are the bomb (♠2 ♥2 ♣2 ♦2) or passing.\n\nThe bomb (four 2s) is a powerful move that beats any non-rocket combination, including the current play. Using it here would immediately take control of the trick and allow me to lead next, which is highly advantageous as the landlord. However, bombs are scarce and valuable — especially since rockets (the only thing that can beat them) are limited to the two jokers. I hold the Black Joker but not the Red Joker (still unplayed, count shows 1 remaining). That means a rocket (both jokers) is still possible if the opponent has the Red Joker.\n\nBut consider: if I don’t bomb now, I must pass. Passing gives up the lead and allows the next player to play first in the new round. As the landlord, losing initiative frequently leads to being locked down by coordinated peasant plays. With a strong hand and a chance to regain lead, playing the bomb now ensures I get to play next. After I bomb, I can lead with a small card or set up a chain to burn through my hand quickly.\n\nAlso note: I have BlackJoker + one 2 left after playing the bomb, which could combine later into a potential rocket if needed (though unlikely unless I get lucky). But more importantly, holding onto the bomb risks wasting its value — if later plays are small, bombing becomes inefficient.\n\nThus, the optimal move is to play the bomb now and seize the lead.\n\nReason: The only way to beat the current six-card sequence is with a bomb or rocket. I can play a bomb (four 2s), which will win the trick and give me the next lead — a critical advantage for the landlord. Passing surrenders initiative and lets opponents control the pace. Given that I have few high-power combos otherwise and need to push toward emptying my hand, using the bomb here maximizes strategic advantage.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♠2", "♥2", "♣2", "♦2"]}'}
