ote: playing a high single like BlackJoker now could be inefficient — it will likely win, but it only clears one card, and I’ll lose initiative if they follow with something I can’t beat easily.\n\nSimilarly, playing a weak pair like 4s gives them an easy chance to overplay and continue their turn.\n\nThus, the best balance is to play a **high pair** that pressures them and maximizes chance of retaining lead.\n\nBetween Ks and As — **Aces are higher**, so safer.\n\nAlso, I hold both Aces → playing them removes two key high cards from my hand and forces opponents to use bombs or rockets to beat it (which they may not want to waste).\n\nReason:  \nPlaying the pair of Aces (♠A ♣A) is optimal because:\n- It\'s a high-ranking pair unlikely to be beaten without a bomb or rocket.\n- It efficiently clears two high cards from my hand.\n- It maintains pressure on the peasants who likely want to preserve bombs/rockets for critical moments.\n- After this play, if both pass, I get another free turn — possibly allowing me to play another strong combo.\n\nSaving BlackJoker for now is wise — I can use it later as a finisher or in response to a bomb.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♠A", "♣A"]}'}
